---
// Location page (DESIGN_BRIEF.md section 4). Exists for search, lives in the
// footer rather than the main nav, and **does not conflict with the
// no-geography project-naming rule** — section 4 says so explicitly. Project
// names stay placeless for brand reasons; the site still has to be findable.
// Nothing on this page names a project, a client, or an address.
//
// Keyword target (8.3): "window treatments Cincinnati OH" — title, H1, first
// paragraph, meta description. Once each, per 8.3's own "do not stuff" rule.
//
// Neighbourhoods (8.4): the brief's instruction is to name the areas
// *naturally within the two location pages*, and explicitly NOT to build
// twelve thin doorway pages, which Google penalises. The Cincinnati-side
// names from 8.4's list appear here; the Kentucky-side ones are on
// /northern-kentucky, so neither page is a duplicate of the other.
//
// **Nothing here claims a project in a named neighbourhood.** The brief
// establishes these as where Amy's clients are concentrated, which supports
// naming them as ground she covers — it does not establish a finished job on
// any particular street, and inventing one would break rule 4 and be untrue.
//
// Vendor naming: rule 8's "the lines I represent." Lafayette is named on its
// own page only. No pricing language (rule 1).
//
// Image: the standing IMAGE-MANIFEST.md placeholder rule — a real photograph,
// never a blank box. Swap when Kelsee's shoot lands.

import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RevealImage from '../components/RevealImage.astro';
import { photos } from '../data/photos';

const lead = photos['banded-roman-shade'];
---

<Base
  title="Window Treatments Cincinnati OH | Amy K Clark Design"
  description="Custom window treatments in Cincinnati OH: drapery, blinds and shades, upholstery, and soft furnishings, designed, made, and installed by Amy Clark."
  ogImage="/images/og/og-default.jpg"
  transparentNav={false}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { name: 'Cincinnati', href: '/cincinnati' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="section wrap text-page__intro">
    <p class="type-label text-page__eyebrow">Service area</p>
    <h1 class="type-display text-page__heading">Window Treatments in Cincinnati</h1>
    <p class="text-page__lead">
      Custom window treatments in Cincinnati OH, made by the person who measured the room. Amy
      designs the treatment, sews it in her own workroom, and hangs it herself, which is a
      different arrangement from the one most of this city offers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <RevealImage
    src={lead.image}
    alt={lead.alt}
    blurDataURL={lead.blurDataURL}
    aspectRatio="16/9"
    sizes="100vw"
  />

  <section class="section wrap text-page__body">
    <h2 class="type-display text-page__subhead">Where she works</h2>
    <p>
      Hyde Park, Indian Hill, Mariemont, Terrace Park, Wyoming, and Anderson Township are regular
      ground, and so is everything between them. The houses are not interchangeable and neither
      are the windows. A 1920s Hyde Park sunroom with original steel casements is a different
      problem from a new build in Anderson with a two-storey wall of glass, and the same treatment
      would be wrong in both.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display text-page__subhead">What that means in an older house</h2>
    <p>
      Older Cincinnati houses rarely give you a square opening. Plaster moves, sills are out of
      level, and the trim was run by hand a century ago. Every measurement gets taken in the room
      rather than assumed off a plan, and the treatment is built to the opening that is actually
      there. That is the part that decides whether an inside mount reads as intentional or as
      slightly off, and it is why nothing is ordered from a photograph.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display text-page__subhead">The whole textile layer</h2>
    <p>
      Drapery, blinds and shades, upholstery, pillows and cushions. Fabric comes from the lines I
      represent, chosen for how it will hang and wear rather than for how it looks folded on a
      table. Amy is not a full-service interior design firm and does not pretend to be one: she
      owns the textile layer of a room and works alongside whoever owns the rest.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a designer, builder, or project manager is already running the house, that is an ordinary
      way to work here. If it is just you and a room that has never looked right, that is ordinary
      too.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display text-page__subhead">Start with the room</h2>
    <p>
      Every project starts with a consultation in the house, because the light, the sightlines,
      and the way the room actually gets used are the things that decide the work. The
      <a href="/process" class="link-underline">process</a> page walks through what happens after
      that, and the <a href="/portfolio" class="link-underline">portfolio</a> shows finished work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap contact-band bg-ground">
    <p class="type-display contact-band__line">Ready to start your project?</p>
    <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-solid">Schedule a Consultation</a>
  </section>
</Base>
